(Phnom Penh): On Tuesday evening, March 7, 2023, the Prime Minister of Cambodia Samdech Techo Hun Sen will preside over the closing ceremony of the Ministry of Health. The ceremony was held at Sokha Hotel, Phnom Penh.
Cambodia is making progress towards achieving the national goal of eradicating malaria by 2025. The malaria rate has dropped from four cases per 1,000 population to 0.24 cases. The death toll from malaria has dropped to 0 from 2018 to 2022.
* Non-communicable disease prevention and treatment services are constantly being expanded, such as services for diabetes, high blood pressure, cervical cancer research, etc.
* The Equity Fund for Poor Families and Informal Workers program extends coverage to other target populations, including the implementation of cash subsidies for pregnant women and children under the age of two.
The meeting also noted the progress made in strengthening the capacity and resilience of the health system, including:
Kovid 19 response has become an opportunity to strengthen the health system.
* Between 2018-2022, the national budget allocated for the health sector increased by 5.1%. Expenditures at both the national and capital-provincial levels are geared towards supporting the process of providing health services, developing and expanding health services, and improving quality. Expenditure on medicines, vaccines, reactors, medical devices and technology has increased significantly, especially in 2021 during the Kovid-19 phase.
* Health Information Database, Disease Monitoring and Reporting System, Patient Registration System, Human Resource Management System, Laboratory, Logistics and Finance Improved Performance The Ministry has completed the development of the Digital Health Strategy 2022-2033 and established a Development Steering Committee. Digital health system at the Ministry of Health in late 2022.
* Between 2018 and 2022, pharmaceutical importers, importers of cosmetics, traditional medicine shops and pharmacies that do not comply with the law on drug control were fined 540 cases and closed 347 private service locations in violation of the law. That is, in some cases, court proceedings are required.
Pursuant to Sub-Decree No. 193 dated December 4, 2019, the Ministry of Health has transferred the management and provision of health services to the 25 capital-provincial administrations from January 1, 2020. The Ministry has also delegated the regulatory function in granting business licenses to private health services to the OWSO at the capital-provincial and municipal-district-khan levels for many years.
