A Phnom Penh Municipal Court judge on March 3, 2023, announced the verdict in the case of Kem Sokha's treason after holding 66 hearings.
The verdict sentenced Kem Sokha to 27 years in prison for conspiracy with a foreigner or conspiracy with a foreigner in Cambodia and elsewhere between 2010 and September 3, 2017, as a felony. And shall be punished in accordance with the provisions of Article 439 and Article 443 of the Penal Code.
Mr. Kem Sokha was also sentenced to an additional sentence, permanently depriving him of the right to vote, the right to stand for election, and the right to engage in other political activities under Article 450 of the Penal Code.
The verdict against Kem Sokha received strong support from the Cambodian people, government officials and the armed forces.
Pong Phea, a fourth-year law student, said the Phnom Penh Municipal Court's verdict demonstrated the strengthening of the rule of law in Cambodia and the strengthening of Cambodia's independence and sovereignty as an independent state.
Pong Phea said that as a result of adhering to the rule of law, there is nothing better than a court case. The young man spoke of Cambodia's past, and those accused of treason were not given such an opportunity to advocate, but would be executed immediately.
Pong Phea said, "Through 66 hearings, this is a huge opportunity for Kem Sokha and his lawyer to defend themselves in court. In particular, in the past of Cambodia, those who were accused of treason did not get such an opportunity to advocate, but were killed. "The case of Kem Sokha is a huge opportunity for our country, which is firmly implementing the rule of law."
Ly Siv Hour also supported the court's verdict. Support because before the sentencing decision, the court gave Mr. Kem Sokha and his lawyer the opportunity to present and present evidence to acquit. But through the video that Mr. Kem Sokha spoke from Australia as strong evidence of acts of treason that the law can not forgive.
According to the Constitution of Cambodia, all individuals must be treated equally and honestly before the law. For Kem Sokha, despite being convicted of a serious crime, he was not taken to prison. He was allowed to stay at home with his family, only to be denied the right to freedom of movement.
For the youth, Pong Phea considered this verdict as a great understanding and favor for Mr. Kem Sokha.
"I think the power of this court's verdict is a great favor for Kem Sokha," he said. Normally, the rule of law must be equal, all must be imprisoned by the power of the verdict. In particular, Mr. Kem Sokha is a big favor that just closed some rights such as the right to travel and political rights. "But he can have normal relationships in his home."
Kem Sokha was convicted in a video clip he spoke in Australia in 2014. In this video, he shows some of the action plans to overthrow the government, which he has been working on since 1993 with the help of foreigners, modeled on Yugoslavia and Serbia.
Cambodia had a model of such a revolution once through the coup of March 18, 1970. That coup plunged Cambodia into the flames of war for nearly three decades. Millions of Cambodians have been killed, injured, disabled, deprived of their country and incalculable.
Legal action against Kem Sokha is a timely step to prevent Cambodia from falling into the abyss of war as in the past.
Pong Phea and Ly Siv Hour fully supported the action of the authorities.
"If his government or our competent authorities do not take timely action, our country may fall into the flames of war like the countries affected by the color revolution," he said. Like Cambodia in the past. "This is a timely step to maintain peace, political stability for our national development."
"If we look at the past, our country has had color revolutions to overthrow the government and cause many problems and negative effects in our society," she said. "This is the right thing to do to ensure security, public order, the well-being of the people, especially to ensure the country's development."
Responding to the reaction and statement of the US Embassy in Cambodia, Mr. Yan Vuthea questioned why the US Ambassador on behalf of a democracy does not support the legal process? Or want to support the color revolution they have done in the past.
Mr. Yan Vuthea called on the United States not to interfere in Cambodia's internal affairs, not to support the color revolution that will bring the United States into the wrong again for the people and the country.
