MANILA (Reuters) - Authorities believe they will be able to locate the tanker that sank in the middle of the sea last week as they step up efforts to assess the extent of the damage, the Philippine Ministry of Environment said on Monday. Oil spills and the extent of further environmental damage. This is according to the release by the AFP news agency on Monday, March 6, 2023.
The statement added that remote-controlled autonomous vehicles would be deployed to help locate the tanker.
Experts said authorities wanted to know how much oil was inside the vessel and how to pump it out or stop the leak.
According to the Coast Guard, the ship was carrying about 800,000 liters of industrial oil when it had an engine problem at sea on Feb. 28.
It was not immediately clear what caused the sinking of the Philippine flag, but all 20 crew members were rescued before it sank.
The disaster agency said the oil spill was spotted on the coast and in coastal waters near more than 60 villages near where the ship was believed to have sunk.
About 36,000 hectares of coral reefs and seagrass are likely to be affected by the oil layer, said marine scientists at the University of the Philippines.
