The NEC of Cambodia and the Palestinians are confident that they will continue to share their experiences and best practices through coordination from JICA.
After the two sides exchanged greetings, Tep Nytha briefed the Palestinian delegation on the history of the Cambodian election.
Mr. Tep Nytha emphasized that since 2015, Cambodia has made in-depth reforms in its elections, including the National Election Committee in the Constitution. Members of the NEC are elected by the National Assembly.
He said the NEC had reformed voter registration by changing from manual registration to computerized registration. He added that in addition, the NEC has developed the election to be easier and more modern through the introduction:
Program to prepare the list of political parties to stand for election and the list of candidates to stand for election * Program to issue identification documents to serve the election (EE) List of Elected Candidates * Case Manager and Complaint Report
The Secretary General of the NEC confirmed that in 2023, Cambodia is holding two elections, the 7th National Assembly election and the newly formed and newly formed municipal and district council elections.
After listening carefully to the remarks of the Secretary General of the NEC of Cambodia, the Palestinian NEC delegation highly appreciated the voter registration in Cambodia, which is more than 80 percent. The Palestinian delegation also observed and appreciated the continuing voter education courses that the NEC of Cambodia, in collaboration with JICA, has been organizing in educational institutions to spread awareness about democracy and youth elections.
Japan Provides Over $ 300,000 for Rural Education and Rehabilitation in Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): The Royal Government of Japan has agreed to provide a total of more than $ 300,000 to three grant recipients for the implementation of rural education and rehabilitation programs for the disabled in Cambodia under the Japan Economic Cooperation Grant Program for small-scale projects. Human Security, or Kusano in Japanese.
The grant agreement was signed by Japanese Ambassador to Cambodia U Inu Assushi and representatives of the three recipients on the afternoon of March 6, 2023.
First, $ 120,768 will be provided to Oddar Meanchey Provincial Department of Education, Youth and Sports for the construction of a school building with 5 classrooms equipped with school furniture at Rumduol Primary School in Banteay Ampil district, which will benefit Approximately 220 students and 9 teachers through improving the quality of education.
Second, $ 91,010 was provided to Kratie Provincial Department of Education, Youth and Sports for the construction of a school building at Phnom Rol Primary School in Prek Prosop District with 5 classrooms, 1 toilet, 3 classrooms and installation of school furniture. Classes such as tables, chairs and blackboards will benefit a total of 266 students and 7 teachers.
And the third: more than $ 89.001 thousand for the project to purchase 11 machines for the production of prosthetics and physical therapy equipment at the National Rehabilitation Center for the Handicapped in Kien Khleang in Phnom Penh. The project will benefit 900 people a year who need prosthetics, satellites or rehabilitation services at the center.
On that occasion, the Ambassador of Japan said: "It is gratifying to see that Cambodia has been developing rapidly recently. However, we also recognize that there are still some challenges that need to be addressed to improve people's quality of life. "Therefore, Japan will continue to contribute to addressing these challenges through various official development assistance schemes, including Kusano."
Japan's Kusano grant began in Cambodia in 1991 to support Cambodia's efforts to develop and rebuild the country at the grassroots level. This assistance aims to protect vulnerable people from factors such as poverty or misfortune that could directly threaten their lives, livelihoods and dignity, as well as promote community self-reliance.
Since 1991, the Government of Japan has provided more than $ 66 million to local authorities and NGOs to implement 702 Kusanoni projects throughout Cambodia.
