China to deepen financial reform, open to more foreign investment (Video inside) (Beijing) - Chinese Premier Li Keqiang said on Sunday that China will deepen financial reform and improve the law. To hedge the economy and open up more foreign investment. According to Reuters.
Reuters quoted the outgoing prime minister as saying in his work report for the opening session of the annual session of the Chinese National People's Congress that the Chinese government will hedge risks for real estate companies. High quality and reduce interest payment burden for local government.
"We need to make in-depth reforms of the financial system, improve financial regulation and see that all stakeholders have full responsibility to protect against financial risks," he said. "Local and systematic."
China has stepped up its efforts to tackle financial risks as its economy grew just 3 percent last year, the slowest pace in decades. The economy has been hit by three-year austerity measures to curb Kovid-19 crisis, a real estate crisis, a crackdown on private enterprises and declining demand for Chinese exports.
The Prime Minister also stressed institutional reform compared to last year. The move comes after state media reported on Tuesday that President Xi Jinping planned to redesign state-owned enterprises (SOEs) and Communist Party units.
Two sources told Reuters that Xi, who will remain president for an unprecedented third term, plans to re-establish the Central Financial Affairs Commission. This reflects Xi's push for increased oversight of the financial sector.
To boost economic growth, China's National Development and Reform Commission (NDRC) also said on Sunday that China will push for reforms in key areas and continue to open up to foreign investment.
"We will carry out significant reform work to eliminate institutional barriers to development," the NDRC said.
