(Phnom Penh): Experts in food processing and exporting to international markets have encouraged business owners, industries and handicrafts that produce and process food to strengthen their production to achieve quality standards that are easy to reach. Exporting and gaining the trust of both domestic and international consumers.
This push comes as Cambodia's export market is growing, as Cambodia now has free trade agreements with China, Korea and the RCEP agreement, which is an opportunity for manufacturers to export their products to international markets.
Oknha Keo Mom, owner of Lily Crispy Company, told Fresh News that the more standards a company or enterprise receives, the more benefits it will provide to producers and boost sales. Broad to both domestic and international markets.
"Obviously, the production of standardized lily cakes has boosted exports to many countries, about 10 countries, because we have a clear standard," she said. "Therefore, if other industries intend to export, how should they strive to obtain a certificate that confirms this standard, then the international market is interested in products that promote exports."
She said that in order to get the standard certification, it is necessary to organize the number of employees, record the products and production, especially increase the quality control from the initial production process to the finished product.
She said that when products are manufactured with the right standards, it will make customers more confident in using local products, because if produced without the right standards, it will make consumers less afraid.
She noted that at present, the number of small and medium-sized handicrafts and industries in the country that have not yet been exported to obtain standards and certificates from the Ministry is still limited. However, they also produce according to personal hygiene standards.
She notes that the reason why these handicrafts are difficult to obtain standards and product quality certifications is due to the high cost of obtaining these standards, which leads to higher costs and less competition. In order to encourage local producers to obtain certification, this standard requires the consideration of the Royal Government as well, especially to facilitate the cost of obtaining the standard is affordable.
The Ministry has issued 58 product and sanitation certificates to ensure the safety and well-being of the public and society, and to contribute to the strengthening of quality and efficient trade or services and to support the small-scale industry and handicrafts. Competitiveness in the domestic market.
"We have been working to improve our certification system to be more appropriate and internationally recognized in order to participate in the implementation of regional and bilateral free trade agreements that have entered into force as agreed between Cambodia," he said. "China and Cambodia-Korea and the RCEP Comprehensive Economic Partnership Agreement."
