Russia's oil exports resilient to Western sanctions
MOSCOW (Reuters) - Russia's offshore oil exports resisted sanctions in February as the country sought new customers despite full Western sanctions. According to Bloomberg News.
The European Union banned Russia's marine fuel imports in early February, two months after tightening crude exports. The sanctions are in addition to setting Russian oil prices, which third countries must follow if they want access to Western services such as shipping and insurance.
Despite the combined effect of the sanctions in February, Russian oil producers exported an average of 7.32 million barrels of crude oil and oil products, Bloomberg reported, citing data from research firm Kpler. Barrels per day. This number is in line with the volume exported in December and is only 9% lower than the record high in January.
