(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Environment together with Dai has selected Pursat province among the six provinces of the Cardamom Mountains to launch the second phase of the "Zero Trap in Cambodia Protected Areas" on April 7, 2023. This face.
The second phase of the Zero Trap Campaign, which will run for five months from March 3 to July 2023, will be implemented in five provinces: Kampong Speu, Pursat, Koh Kong, Battambang, Pailin and Siem Reap.
Speaking at a press conference on the morning of March 2, 2023, Mr. Net Pheaktra, Secretary of State and spokesman for the Ministry of Environment, said that the zero trap campaign will start on March 3, 2023 tomorrow.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra stated that the second phase of the Zero Trap Campaign aims to make people aware of the benefits of wildlife and turn to increased wildlife conservation to provide benefits for eco-tourism as part of Territorial tourism. Although not all traps are removed, this release is also part of the rescue of wildlife.
At the same time, the spokesman of the Ministry of Environment said that in the past two years, from January 1, 2021 to December 31, 2022, officials of the Ministry of Environment, relevant authorities and partner organizations have released a total of traps. Of the 96,357 traps removed, a large number were in the Cardamom Mountains in Koh Kong, Pursat and northeastern provinces such as Mondulkiri, Ratanakkiri, Stung Treng and others such as Preah Vihear, Kampong Thom.
In addition, Mr. Neth Pheaktra said that in the past two years, professional officials and partner organizations have confiscated 1,416 revolvers and automatic rifles.
According to Seng Teak, Executive Director of the Global Fund for the Conservation of Natural Resources (WWF), the first phase of the Zero Trap Campaign in the eastern provinces of the Mekong River has reached more than 3 million people. The importance of wildlife and natural resources through more than 600 news articles published by national and international media.
The president of the organization confirmed that after the first zero trap campaign, the number of traps has decreased significantly. The latest figures show that in 2021, before the zero trapping campaign, the number of traps collected in the protected area was 61,160, and in 2022, after the zero trapping campaign, only 35,118 traps were collected. This figure is down by about 40 percent, especially for recycled guns.
Mr. Seng Teak stated that according to the data provided by the Ministry of Environment in 2021, 1,200 firearms were collected, but in 2022, only 516 were collected, a decrease of 50%. Good results through zero trapping.
Mr. Pheaktra also highlighted that the results of the first zero trap campaign not only inspire people in the target provinces to understand, especially some restaurant owners have stopped using wild animals to cook for guests. Takeo Governor: No change in the statue of "Preah Noray" which is the symbol of the province, the statue of duck eggs and lobster only shows the potential products of the province
