General Knowledge: Japan turns from an empire with one of the strongest military powers to a country without formal armies
(Phnom Penh): If we look at the period between the early 20th century and the middle of the 20th century, Japan was one of the strongest military empires to the point of ambition to occupy the Asia-Pacific region and the world. But now Japan has become a country that does not have an official army like other countries.
At the beginning of the 20th century, Japan went to war with many countries. In 1904, Japan fought Russia. In 1910, Japan annexed the Korean Peninsula. In 1932, the island nation established a puppet state in northeast China. But then Japan crossed the US red line.
After the attack on Pearl Harbor in Hawaii in December 1941 that killed tens of thousands of American sailors, the United States participated in World War II. The United States and Japan fought in the Pacific, where Japan was the loser after the United States dropped two atomic bombs on Hiroshima and Nagasaki, killing about 200,000 people.
The above joint statement was prepared by the United States, the United Kingdom and the Soviet Union. These include restrictions such as the Japanese military must give up all weapons and parts of Japan must remain under the control of Allied forces until there is acceptable evidence that Japan's war-torn powers have been completely disbanded.
The Japanese emperor has stated that this unconditional surrender is necessary to save the lives of millions of other Japanese people who could have been killed by the atomic bomb if Japan continued to wage war. A total of about 3 million Japanese died in World War II. Emperor Hirohito also called on the people to return to peace.
By this time, Japan's military empire had come to an end, and so did Japan's dominance of the Korean Peninsula and its Asia-Pacific ambitions. Two years later, Japan adopted a new constitution, which was drafted within a week by the United States.
These included notable changes, such as a reduction in the power of the emperor. But the most important point is Article 9, which requires Japan to give up the right to declare war forever. As a result, the Japanese army had to be completely disbanded. As a result, Japan has transformed from a former military power to a pacifist country that is no longer capable of warfare.
Since then, the pacifist trend has been deeply rooted in Japanese society, with the East Asian nation focusing only on economic recovery. This attitude continues to this day with some variations.
The Japanese Constitution explicitly states that the Army, Navy and Air Force, as well as other forces capable of waging war, will not be retained. And in the years since the new constitution was adopted, Japan has strictly followed this provision, keeping only the police force, which does not seem to have a properly concentrated structure.
But in 1950, the Korean War broke out when North Korea invaded South Korea to take control of the entire Korean Peninsula. US troops stationed in Japan under Japanese subjugation during World War II were suddenly needed in South Korea.
The birth of the SDF is controversial in Japan, as the US-written constitution explicitly prohibits Japan from retaining any armed forces. However, the SDF is not like the general army, as the SDF's only mission is to protect the people and territory of Japan from external threats. The SDF is banned from attacking other countries if it has not been attacked before.
Generations of Japanese governments have considered that Japan does not have an army, only a bodyguard when needed. The Japanese Supreme Court also upheld the view that SDF forces did not violate Japan's constitution.
However, there were two major events that changed the Japanese public's negative view of the SDF. The first event was related to the 1991 Gulf War. At that time, the United States sent troops to Kuwait to help oust the Iraqi army, while Japan, an ally of the United States, only provided financial support and stood by because the constitution forbade it.
It is a great shame for the Japanese military, as well as for the Japanese people as a whole, as Japan conducts regular military exercises with the United States. Back. Therefore, Japan has found a way by sending SDF forces to participate in overseas missions in the form of UN peacekeeping missions.
In 1992, Japan sent its peacekeepers under the UN umbrella to Cambodia (UNTAC era). It is the first time that Japanese military forces have been deployed abroad since World War II. Since then, Tokyo has deployed military forces around the world, including Africa, the Middle East and Southeast Asia.
It's a smart thing for Japan. The deployment of peacekeeping missions to conflict areas gives Japan the opportunity to play a key role in armed conflict and to partner with allies such as the United States without having to fight with anyone. Such sightings of Japanese military forces abroad have helped restore the SDF's image at home and on the international stage.
However, a change in the attitude of the Japanese people could allow a leader who wants a normal army like other countries to push for change. Former Japanese Prime Minister Shinzo Abe has sought to free Japan from a range of military restrictions.
