This afternoon, Samdech Techo Hun Sen presides over the celebration of the 11th National Clean City Day
(Phnom Penh): On Monday afternoon, February 27, 2023, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, will preside over the celebration of the 11th National Clean City Day.
The 11th National Clean City Day celebration will be held at Sokha Hotel. The National Clean City Day in 2023 is celebrated under the theme "Clean City, Good Hospitality, Hosts Cambodia Provides Warmth".
In response to the theme "Clean city, good hospitality, Cambodian hosts provide warmth", Samdech Techo Prime Minister requested the relevant ministries, institutions, sub-national administration and the people to continue to focus on five priority tasks, including:
First, to continue to ensure peace, security, stability and prosperity, which is the main and most important foundation for building a clean city connected with a smart city that is economically competitive, environmentally sustainable and socially responsible. Implementing the ASEAN Smart Cities Network project to improve the quality of life of the people with well-being, safety, security, prosperity, resilience and provide comfort to national and international tourists.
Second, continue to strengthen and enhance public-private partnerships at the national and sub-national levels to promote sustainable development, urban and urban tourism, responsibility and environment, as well as promote the provision of services. Safe and hospitable.
Fourth, to strengthen the partnership in urban development by promoting national and international cooperation, sharing knowledge, experience and excellence, and mobilizing resources.
Fifth: Encourage the active participation of all citizens by strengthening ownership in the development and maintenance of clean, environmentally friendly, smart and green cities.
Samdech Techo hopes and strongly believes that all stakeholders will continue to cooperate, support and implement all the campaigns launched by the Royal Government to support the Clean City Competition Movement and wish the National Clean City Day and The Clean City Racing Movement is a success.
(Phnom Penh): Ms. O Vandin, Secretary of State and spokesperson of the Ministry of Health, this morning, February 27, 2023, confirmed that the area infected with bird flu in Sithor Kandal district, Prey Veng province, was affected by health officials and relevant authorities. It is completely under control and has not spread to other areas.
In particular, for patients with bird flu who have been infected from their children until now, through the results of laboratory tests, it is said that he has a negative disease (cured), but the medical team gave the patient to take all the medicine. Limited number and continue to monitor health with the highest attention. This is according to what Ms. O Vandin emphasized.
A spokesman for the Ministry of Health claimed that a total of 16 people were affected, including 12 with the dead girl and four with the father, and laboratory results confirmed that they were negative for the flu. Birds, but doctors took samples from another group of 13 suspected flu-like symptoms with the same negative results.
Although the situation is under control, there is no spread of the disease, but Ms. O Vandin continued to urge all people to continue to pay the highest attention to the spread of avian influenza. The H5N1. She emphasizes that the disease, in case of infection, progresses to a more serious condition and leads to more death than Kovid 19.
Please be reminded that in the morning of February 24, 2023, the Ministry of Health issued a statement that after the death of an 11-year-old girl on February 22, 2023, living in Roleang village, Romlech commune, Sithor Kandal district, Prey Veng province. The Ministry of Health's emergency investigation team, in collaboration with local authorities and Prey Veng Provincial Health Department, together with health partners, arrived at the base immediately on February 23, 2023.
The Ministry of Health claims that this is the second case in February 2023 after the virus was not found in humans for the past 9 years in the Kingdom of Cambodia, the last case is the 58th case. Found on March 23, 2023, there were 45 cases of children under 14 years of age and 31 cases out of 58 cases were women.
International aid agencies need the funds to help the more than 17 million people in Yemen who have been devastated by the eight-year civil war. The conflict has claimed hundreds of thousands of lives and plunged the poorest country on the Arabian Peninsula into one of the world's worst humanitarian tragedies.
In 2015, a Saudi-led coalition launched an intervention campaign to support the Yemeni government after Houthi rebels seized control of the capital, Sanaa, along with much of Yemen. In 2022, the UN raised more than $ 2.2 billion, allowing aid agencies to reach nearly 11 million people across Yemen every month.
