(Phnom Penh): Many countries and peoples around the world, including the Royal Government and the people of Cambodia, have come together to help the Turkish people who are suffering severely from the earthquake.
Turkey is a Muslim country and used to have close ties with Khmer-Muslims. Through various humanitarian organizations, Turkish citizens have also been involved in humanitarian activities with the Cambodian Muslim community in Cambodia.
When there is an earthquake in Turkey and the people are in need of that help. The Khmer Muslim community has also been raising funds to help Turkish citizens.
Seth Mohamad Sis, Undersecretary of State at the Council of Ministers, recently raised funds from the Cambodian Muslim community, as well as former students in Turkey, to raise $ 1,466.68 to a Turkish charity, CANSUYU. .
Pictures of the distribution of food packages highlighted by the Cambodian flag to the people of Turkey have attracted a lot of attention on Facebook. These images will convey to the people of Turkey and will also remind them of Cambodia's generous activities as part of strengthening the relationship between the people of the two countries in line with the policies of the Royal Government of Cambodia and the Government of Turkey.
Seth Mohammed Sisi said this was the first aid package and he would continue to raise funds to address the plight of Turkish people in need.
It should be noted that the Royal Government and the Cambodian Red Cross have provided a total of 200,000 dollars to Turkey to alleviate the difficulties of the Turkish government and people affected by the earthquake.
Apart from the Royal Government and the Red Cross, many other private sectors in Cambodia have also joined together to share the difficulties of the Turkish government.
The Turkish government, through its embassy, expresses its deep gratitude to Cambodia for its generosity.
"Cambodia has responded quickly as a strong friend and partner of Turkey," the Turkish ambassador to Cambodia said.
"On behalf of my Embassy and the Turkish community in Cambodia, I would like to express my deep gratitude to the Cambodian people for helping us by all means, as well as the diplomatic community, for their strong solidarity," she said. The message of support and assistance I received was overwhelming. "I consider myself very lucky to have played such a role in a country with such a big heart."
Cambodians in the past have swum through hardships and famines caused by the fires of war and national disintegration. This kind of food package used to be received by Cambodia from other nations at that time. But Cambodians today have become able to help other nations. This is a demonstration of great progress and a source of pride for the Cambodian nation.
