Turkish government issues regulations for reconstruction after quake kills more than 43,000 (Video inside) (Ankara): A sub-decree issued by the Turkish presidential office on Friday said that Turkey Regulations have been put in place for reconstruction work following a devastating earthquake in February. This is according to the publication by the AFP news agency on Friday, February 24, 2023.
According to the sub-decree, individuals, institutions and organizations will be able to build houses and workplaces to donate to the Ministry of Urban Planning and then be distributed to people affected by the earthquake. Dry land and non-forest land can also be used for this construction. Separately, temporary or permanent settlements will also be determined by the ministry according to the seismic risk level.
More than 160,000 buildings with 520,000 apartments collapsed or were severely damaged by an earthquake that killed more than 43,000 people in Turkey and left millions homeless. Homeless people live in cold winter weather.
The 3rd Summit on Sustainable Agriculture 2023 was held in Siem Reap
(Siem Reap): On February 24, 2023, the 3rd Summit on Sustainable Agriculture was held in Siem Reap under the theme "Together to expand solidarity to extract sustainable resources for agricultural development. Efficient and Resistant to Climate ”with about 400 participants, farmers, agribusiness, public, private and development partners in agriculture and food.
The main purpose of this summit is to mobilize immediate participation in the protection and prevention of the risks that are threatening the planet and to find solutions for the continued survival of all of us.
The summit was chaired by Mr. Yang Saing Koma, Minister Delegate to the Prime Minister and Secretary of State for the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, Dr. Mey Kalyan, Senior Advisor of the Supreme National Economic Council and a member of the CACC Board, and Mr. Dr. David Ray, Director of the Cambodia-Australia Partnership for Sustainable Economic Development.
Cambodia has been implementing the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development. However, the need for climate resilience for agro-food systems still requires practical implementation and sustainable solutions to reduce greenhouse gas emissions.
The Summit on Sustainable Agriculture highlighted the initiatives of smart agriculture, such as the use of organic fertilizers through biomass vermiculture and composting, and the use of innovative solar and non-fossil fuels. Water management and for alternative energy, as well as the practice of replanting agriculture.
A representative of the Royal University of Agriculture stated, "The basic link in the food system, the link between agriculture, nutrition and health is important knowledge. We have to try something by researching and then creating something new. "The practice of replanting agriculture can work in harmony with nature and the environment, so it will be a viable and influential solution for change."
