(Phnom Penh): The National Bank of Cambodia, the Central Bank of Solomon Islands and the Central Bank of Fiji have signed two Memoranda of Understanding (MoUs) to strengthen cooperation on financial innovation and payment systems with the Central Bank of Solomon Islands and the Central Bank of Fiji. .
Mr. Chea Chanto said that the signing of the MoU is an important event for the three central banks to achieve a better level of financial cooperation through the use of technology to benefit To the people in each country.
"The main purpose of the MoU is to strengthen cooperation in the development of financial innovation and payment systems, as well as to share relevant information on innovation and digital payments for financial services," he said. "This memorandum of understanding will provide a framework for promoting cooperation through the exchange of knowledge and experience with each other."
Luke Forau, Governor of the Solomon Islands Central Bank, expressed his pleasure at cooperating with the National Bank of Cambodia to learn about Cambodia's experience in using technology to benefit the people of Solomon Islands.
"Cambodia has provided an opportunity for us to understand the leadership of the National Bank of Cambodia on the development of financial technology, as well as to provide additional support through the signing of a Memorandum of Understanding on cooperation in the development of possible payment systems," he said. "Let the public make payments and get funding easily and immediately."
Luke Forau Invites Chea Chanto to Visit the Solomon Islands to Attend the 38th South Pacific Central Bank Governors' Meeting in Honolulu to Share Cambodia's Experience with Other Member States From the South Pacific.
Mr. Esala Masitabua, Deputy Governor of the Central Bank of Fiji, thanked the National Bank of Cambodia for its experience and knowledge in using technology to develop payment systems.
Please be informed that the Bakong System of the National Bank of Cambodia is a project that uses platform technology to improve connectivity and cross-institutional operations to strengthen the efficiency of the payment system and to contribute to the improvement of the current financial environment and the use of the riel. The Bakong system has 70 member institutions.
On the afternoon of February 21, 2023, Japan, through the Embassy of Japan in ASEAN Member States, hosted an online conference to discuss two key topics, including 50 years of progress in ASEAN-Japan relations; An exchange program led by the Japan-ASEAN Foundation.
