Takeo Documentation Center Continues Community Education Forum at Wat Khnarok Koh
(Takeo): A community education forum on remembering the history of the Khmer Rouge, organized by the Takeo Provincial Documentation Center in collaboration with local authorities in Doung Khpos commune and Wat Khnarok Koh committee, was held at Wat Khnarok Koh, Ta Sai village, Doung Khpos commune. Borey Chulsa district, Takeo province, on the morning of February 18, 2023.
With the participation of 16 monks, abbots, pagoda committee and survivors of the Khmer Rouge regime, as well as 20 students from Sok An Dong High School. The purpose of this educational forum is to establish an intergenerational discussion on the history of the Khmer Rouge and the prevention of violence at the individual and community levels. The forum also discusses current challenges in the community.
The choice of venue for the forum at Wat Khnarok Koh was due to the fact that the pagoda had a history of being a Khmer Rouge hospital during the early 1970s, and that the dilapidated church was a salt shaker during the Khmer Rouge regime.
Mr. Tes Navy, High Commune Councilor, explained the reason for holding the forum at this time as to establish a discussion on Khmer Rouge history and encouraged Khmer Rouge survivors to share their stories. The next generation of young people to remember and encourage all young people to pay attention to listen and record important events that happened in the personal lives of each survivor.
Venerable Borey Chulsar Sub-District Venerable Soy Vuthy instructed all young people to be honest and listen to the explanations and sharing experiences of the Khmer Rouge regime by the survivors of the Khmer Rouge regime.
Mr. Pheng Pong Rasy once again reminded all generations of young people about some important ways to prevent violence that could lead to genocide in the future. Mr. Pheng Pong Rasy presented some tips that young people can participate in preventing violence at the individual level, which is the root of participation in building and developing the country without war.
The younger generation, who are also students, have shown a keen interest in listening to the stories of all the survivors. Two student representatives who expressed their interest expressed sympathy for the survivors and worked hard to learn more to help prevent violence.
Mr. N Chhoeun, Deputy Chief of Roteh Phluk Village, recommended to all students to pay more attention to study, especially to learn deeply about the bitter history that happened to their ancestors. Only learning can eliminate violence and murder. Therefore, you must strive to learn and understand in depth our history, especially the story that happened to your own family.
Mr. Panh Pal, a resident of Ta Sai village, Doung Khpos commune, Borey Chulsar district, described that after the 1970 coup d'etat, in 1971 and 1972, all the monks living in this pagoda were evacuated from pagodas without knowing where they were evacuated. . I also did not stick, but just know that this pagoda is used as a hospital of the Khmer Liberation, called District Hospital 108.
Suon Det, a 17-year-old male student, said: "I was very interested when I heard my grandparents talk about the history of the Khmer Rouge. I am one hundred percent convinced that the Khmer Rouge regime really did exist. I feel sorry for my grandparents who had a bitter experience through the Khmer Rouge regime. I will work hard to learn to help develop our society.
"I was devastated when I heard about the history of Cambodia in the past, which reflects the bitterness of the Cambodian people," said 18-year-old student Chuong Ly Ee. As a student in 12th grade, I am determined to study hard for a better future and for the nation to prosper. "I do not make the nation fall and decline like in the past."
