135-Year-Old Preah Theat Pagoda Rehabilitated and Renovated Today, CPP Officials Celebrate Crossing and Burial Ceremony
(Siem Reap): Due to the age of the temple of Wat Preah Theat, which is 135 years old, this pagoda has been continuously repaired and improved. In fact, in the last three years, the Chief Inspector of the pagoda committee and the people near and far have repaired and renovated at a cost of nearly 40,000 dollars.
The border crossing and burial ceremony was presided over by Oknha Seang Nam, a Siem Reap constituency lawmaker from the Cambodian People's Party and chairman of the provincial working group to help Kralanh district on February 20, 2023.
On that occasion, the monk Monivit Wai, the chief abbot of Wat Preah Theat, said that the temple of Wat Preah Sariktheat called Wat Preah Theat is 17 meters long and 12 meters wide and 6 meters high in Preah Theat village, Khvav commune, Chi Kreng district, Siem Reap province. It is 153 years old and is a monastery built on the site of an ancient temple built in the late 15th century. From the construction of the pagoda to the present, this pagoda is remembered for having 22 chief inspectors.
Funds raised to renovate the temple came from the contribution of Buddhists at the foot of the pagoda and Buddhists near and far, and philanthropists near and far, and the leadership of the Kathina and other festivals of the Chief Inspector and the pagoda committee. He said the pagoda still owed $ 12,555 in construction materials and land.
