(Tbong Khmum): Youth in Tonlong commune participated in a weekend forum on the daily life of the Steang indigenous people in Memot district during the Khmer Rouge regime organized by the Koh Thmor Documentation Center.
This forum was held on February 19, 2023 with the participation of 26 young people, mostly indigenous people from Stlong commune, Memot district, Tbong Khmum province.
Mr. Long Dany, Director of Koh Thmor Documentation Center, led the youth to visit the exhibition of 133 photographs, drawings and maps in the office of Koh Thmor Documentation Center. Afterwards, Mr. Long Dany gave a presentation on the history of Democratic Kampuchea (1975-1979) on the daily lives of the Stieng indigenous people in Memot district during the Khmer Rouge regime.
Cooperative formation: Cooperative is a place where people live together, work together, eat together and share entertainment as a group. The Khmer Rouge established cooperatives as part of the elimination of private ownership, capitalism, and the strengthening of the peasantry and workers. In 1975, Koh Thmor and other villages in Tonlong commune, Memot district, were established by the Khmer Rouge as cooperatives in late 1975 and early 1976.
Violations of children's rights and labor: Under the Khmer Rouge regime, Indigenous children were separated from their parents and forced to do other labor, such as lifting cow dung, cutting down trees, and encroaching on rice fields. Learning lasts about an hour a day.
Forced labor: Indigenous peoples, as well as Cambodians throughout the country, were forced by the Khmer Rouge to work in the fields for more than 12 hours a day, seven days a week, and without food. Enough.
Mr. Long Dany made additional presentations on the prevention of genocide, the use of violence, discrimination and its consequences.
I learned a lot after attending the weekend class forum. Mr. Long Dany made a presentation on each poster, making me aware of new things that I had never known before. Finally, thank you for letting me be here today.
