Malaysia to focus on commodity prices, wage hikes as growth slows
KUALA LUMPUR: Malaysian Prime Minister Anwar Ibrahim is exploring ways to boost wages for Malaysian workers as gloomy economic prospects plague his new administration. According to Bloomberg News.
According to Bloomberg, Economy Minister Rafizi Ramli was quoted as saying at a news conference on Wednesday that Malaysia plans to target wage growth for all workers and formulate policies to support this. He added that the Malaysian National Economic Action Council will meet in March to discuss the issue at a meeting chaired by the prime minister.
Local elections are scheduled to take place amid anticipation of a slowdown in Malaysia's economic growth. The Malaysian government expects economic growth to slow to between 4% and 5% by 2023, following the fastest pace in more than two decades last year due to rising demand.
Chinese oil company resumes Russian crude imports
Russia's Urals crude, which is widely used in Europe, is now being exported to India and China following an EU ban on Russia's crude trade due to the war in Ukraine.
China's state-owned oil refinery has received permission from its parent company to buy Russian crude from oil traders at a sharp discount, which will significantly reduce costs for China, an oil importer. Top crude in the world and increase profits from refining.
Russia's resumption of green crude purchases comes as demand for transportation oil is recovering in China, the world's second-largest oil consumer. From this country ended the Kovid Zero policy.
The data showed the company last imported 730,000 barrels of Urals in October and November.
