Commander-in-Chief of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces receives Commander of the Japanese Army Self-Defense Forces
(Phnom Penh): General Vong Pisen, Commander-in-Chief of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces (RCAF), on the morning of February 15, 2023, received General Yushida Yushidi, Commander of the Japanese Army Self-Defense Forces, and a delegation to pay a courtesy call on the Commander-in-Chief.
On that occasion, General Vong Pisen, on behalf of the Commander-in-Chief of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces, welcomed the visit of General Yushida Yushid and his delegation and hoped that through this visit, good results would be contributed to the expansion. Friendly relations, cooperation between the armies of the two countries.
General Vong Pisen briefed General Yushida Yushid on some of the situation in Cambodia, both during the war and after the war until Cambodia achieved full peace in 1998 and is now enjoying better political stability and development. All sectors.
General Vong Pisen highlighted Cambodia's success in controlling Kovid-19 and reopened the country at the end of 2021, which depends on three main factors: leadership, participation and support, especially leadership. Of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, who implemented an effective and timely policy.
With this, General Vong Pisen also mentioned Cambodia's contribution to the international community, such as maintaining security, political stability, cracking down on transnational crime, terrorism, climate change, as well as participating in maintaining peace, humanitarian work within the framework. United Nations and so on.
He also reminded of Japan's contribution to the search for peace in Cambodia, which helped Cambodia establish a legitimate Royal Government in 1993, and to this day, Japan continues to help develop Cambodia, in which the most important factor is to help build Human resources, helping to build infrastructure, strengthening the peacekeeping force, and helping Cambodian workers to work in Japan.
Regarding the cooperation between the two countries' armies, General Vong Pisen confirmed that the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces will continue to strictly implement the Memorandum of Understanding (MOU) on Cooperation and Military Exchange between the Ministry of National Defense of Cambodia and the Ministry of National Defense of Japan. Signed together in December 2013 in Tokyo.
The Commander-in-Chief thanked the Japanese Self-Defense Force for its past material support to the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces, including the provision of human resource training by providing long-term scholarships for active duty officers and commanders.
On that occasion, the Commander-in-Chief also requested Japan to continue to accept more Cambodian trainees to train in Japan and requested Japan to consider providing medical facilities according to the needs of the Army. Cambodia, which discussed with the Japanese side.
In response, General Yushida Yushidi expressed his warm gratitude for the warm welcome from His Excellency the Commander-in-Chief and highly appreciated the development of the Kingdom of Cambodia under the shadow of peace and leadership of Samdech Akka Moha Sena Padei Techo Hun Sen. That makes Cambodia prosperous.
The General noted the cooperation between the two countries, Cambodia and Japan, which is constantly evolving day by day, in which the defense sector is being strengthened through exchanges of visits and assistance. Training.
General Yushidi expressed his pleasure and congratulations on the 70th anniversary of Cambodia-Japan diplomatic relations, which have reached the level of a comprehensive strategic partnership, and through this success, Japan will continue to strengthen cooperation.
The Commander of the Japanese Army Self-Defense Forces also reported to the Commander-in-Chief on the outcome of talks with Lt. Gen. Hun Manet, Deputy Commander-in-Chief and Commander-in-Chief of the Army, which enhanced the two countries' armies. Closer, especially the expansion of humanitarian aid and disaster relief work.
