Japan's economy escapes fourth-quarter recession but slows growth
TOKYO (Reuters) - Japan's economy avoided a recession but recovered slower than expected in the fourth quarter as business investment slumped.
Reuters quoted analysts as saying that although private consumption is helping to keep pace with rising living costs, uncertainty about the global economic outlook will weigh on the recovery. Of Japan's economy from the Kovid-19 pandemic.
The world's third-largest economy grew 0.6 percent year-on-year in the last quarter of last year, after declining 1 percent in the third quarter, Japanese government data showed on Tuesday. 3.
The increase in gross domestic product (GDP) was lower than market estimates of 2% due to lower capital and inventory spending.
"We can expect consumption to increase as service costs stabilize," he said. "But it is difficult to predict a strong recovery, in part due to the pressure from rising inflation."
Minister of Labor: Development of working conditions and harmonization of industrial relations is a work priority that the Ministry must work together to achieve
Mr. Ith Sam Heng, Minister of Labor and Vocational Training, said this at the opening ceremony of the workshop on the guidelines on the limits of joint labor dispute resolution on the morning of February 15, 2023 at the Ministry of Labor. .
Mr. Ith Sam Heng stated that the mission of the Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training is to ensure compliance with the work and benefits of workers, as well as to provide a sustainable and resilient and resilient work and investment environment to continue to attract investment and Order internationally and maintain a reputation as a country that produces clean products that do not violate the rights and working conditions.
Therefore, the organization of this workshop is very important to build the capacity of labor inspectors and labor dispute mediators to be prepared to prevent and resolve any phenomena that may occur in various enterprises in a timely and efficient manner. Effectively aims to ensure the benefits of workers and maintain business stability.
While the Joint Labor Inspection Team has achieved many results, it sees a number of challenges that need to be addressed and addressed, especially the definition of jurisdiction in resolving labor disputes, strikes and demonstrations. Occurs in enterprises and establishments in the provinces as well as in Phnom Penh, the capital. In this regard, the Minister has introduced and provided guidance for resolving these cases as follows:
1. Must follow the guidelines on procedures for resolving labor disputes, strikes and demonstrations properly, effectively, effectively and responsibly.
2. Must be proactive in resolving all issues in industrial relations (labor inspections, resolving labor disputes, strikes, demonstrations) in accordance with its jurisdiction in a spirit of close cooperation, without allowing any disputes or labor disputes to be dragged out Prolonged settlement to the detriment of public order and national security.
3. Must continue to implement the campaign of a factory, an enterprise as a safe community based on the following criteria: Good working conditions (better than the law) 2. Have a good professional relationship 3. Safe and healthy work 4. There is a program to improve productivity and 5. There is a social responsibility program.
4. Continue to resolve labor disputes procedurally on the basis of law, honesty, transparency, impartiality and professionalism.
5. Strictly and strictly implement all relevant laws and regulations strategically.
6. Ensure that the implementation of all measures is carefully considered on the basis of the law in force to serve the common interest to ensure the harmonization of industrial relations, which is a key factor in attracting investment and keeping jobs for workers.
7. Must continue to participate in the implementation of the 10 recommendations of the Prime Minister. China, US to hold first debt resolution meeting
Officials from China, India, Saudi Arabia and the Group of Seven (G7) nations will attend their first online meeting to discuss public debt on Friday. According to Reuters.
Reuters reports, citing three sources familiar with the plan, that the debt meeting will also include officials from countries requesting debt relief under the Group of 20 framework. Ethiopia, Zambia and Ghana and middle-income countries such as Sri Lanka, Suriname and Ecuador are facing debt crises.
The meeting will be chaired by the co-chairs of the International Monetary Fund, the World Bank and India, the current leaders of the G20 finance ministers, and will take place a week before the group's finance ministers. The G20 is scheduled to meet in Bengaluru, India from February 23 to 25.
One of the three sources told Reuters that Brazil, which will lead the G20 next year, will also attend the roundtable.
