Pakistan smuggles arms, mercenaries to Ukraine
ISLAMABAD: The war in Ukraine has entered its 355th day as of Tuesday, February 14, 2023. The evolution of the war continued, while the resilience of some of the center-right fighters also shifted. Today's report shows that Pakistan is a country that has secretly provided assistance to Ukraine with both weapons and mercenaries.
Although millions of people across Pakistan are suffering from severe food shortages and worsening economic crisis, Pakistan is reportedly over sending weapons and other ammunition to help Ukraine fight. Against Russian soldiers.
Media reports in India and the Middle East have exposed Pakistan's political maneuvering from behind the scenes as Russia's bloody war between Russia and Ukraine is expected to reach its climax. Finals in 2023.
Western media reports say Pakistan's assistance to Ukraine will come back with a supply of equipment that helps Pakistan on its way to upgrade its Western military equipment. The aid that Pakistan will be able to get back also includes Ukrainian expertise to assemble its Mi-17 helicopters, the report said.
Pakistan is reportedly underfunded and has no budget to pay its debts. Pakistan also has no backbone for the possibility of combating rising commodity prices, but when Pakistan was exposed as an armed country and sent more fighters to Ukraine, it made a difference. Surprised by some countries.
In the past, Pakistan has used Russia's cheap oil purchases to counter the price crisis, but Pakistan has been accused of using Russian profits to attack Russia.
The government of Islamabad, which is believed to be at the center of the conflict between Ukraine and Russia, has rejected a UN resolution condemning Russia's attack on Ukraine. Pakistan's refusal at the time provoked strong reactions from the United States and Europe.
The war in Ukraine has entered its 355th day and ends on Tuesday, February 14, 2023. The war between Russia and Ukraine, backed by the United States and the West, has divided the world and forced the country to stand on its own two feet. Could 2023 be the last year of the Ukraine-Russia war resolution?
