MOSCOW (Reuters) - Russia's foreign intelligence service said on Monday that it had received intelligence that the US military was preparing Islamic State militants to attack targets in Russia and the former Soviet Union. This is according to the release by the AFP news agency on Monday, February 13, 2023.
Russia's foreign intelligence service, abbreviated SVR, says 60 militants from ISIS and al Qaeda-linked groups have been recruited and are being trained at US bases in Syria.
However, the agency did not provide evidence of its claims.
Russia's aggression on Ukraine has pushed relations with the United States to its lowest point since the Cold War crisis.
Russian President Vladimir Putin has described the United States as a country that has repeatedly rejected Russia's interests since the collapse of the Soviet Union in 1991. President Joe Biden considers Putin a dictator who poses a serious threat to the United States.
