He said that after the product is branded as a geographical mark, the goods will greatly benefit salt farmers to get more value and promote the official export of salt to the international market, while Kampot Kep salt is now exported to Overseas market, but export in small form through export with Kampot pepper only.
According to the salt producer, the official salt production area is more than 3,700 hectares and there are 200 groups of producers. However, due to the weather, some producers lost money, which forced them to abandon production, leaving only about 139 producers.
He expects that after the product is branded as a geographical brand, it will bring back the producers who abandoned those products to increase production volume even more.
As for salt production this year, he also said that production has encountered some obstacles due to weather conditions, with rain from the beginning of the year, which delayed the harvest until the second week of February. At the same time, labor shortages are a major challenge for salt producers.
