Under the theme "Integration of the economy into development in all areas towards achieving a waste-free society and sustainable development goals", the 11th Asia-Pacific Forum opened a lively discussion with relevant representatives from a number of countries. 39 To achieve results according to the planned agenda.
Mr. Sao Sopheap briefly presented the detailed results of the forum as follows:
1: The First Plenary Meeting on the Establishment of Unity in Achieving the SDGs and the Paris Agreement on Climate Change through Chakra Economy:
The meeting discussed the adoption of economic principles, the Chakra can accelerate progress towards the achievement of the SDGs and the Paris Agreement on Climate Change. Chakra economy focuses on reducing the reuse and recycling of resources can help reduce economic growth from resource use and environmental degradation. This, in turn, could help achieve some of the SDGs, such as poverty reduction, economic growth and climate change.
Governments can collaborate with international organizations and other countries to share best practices and support the implementation of the Chakra economy. In addition, the government can work with sub-national levels, communities and other stakeholders to ensure that chakra economic implementation is integrated into local development plans and responds to local needs and priorities.
2: Second Plenary Meeting on Chakra Economy Towards Sustainable Tourism:
The meeting discussed waste management, which is closely related to the development of tourism, as tourism depends on the attractiveness of clean cities.
3: Third Plenary Meeting on Chakra Economy Towards Sustainable Tonle Sap Ecosystem:
Another important step is to reduce pollution and waste in the Tonle Sap ecosystem. This could include the implementation of regulations and policies that promote cleaner production and consumption, as well as investment in waste management infrastructure and educational programs to promote resource recycling and rehabilitation.
The implementation of economic principles can play an important role in sustaining the Tonle Sap ecosystem by reducing pressure on its resources, promoting sustainable and responsible tourism, reducing pollution and waste, and promoting consumption. Sustainable natural resources.
Establishing environmentally friendly small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs) or enterprise countries involved in mitigation, recycling and recycling will contribute to boosting the economy.
Encouragement from the Royal Government is very important to attract investors to invest in environmentally friendly technology and encourage small and medium enterprise owners to continue their efforts. Government incentives can be made by finding markets, providing recognition, tax breaks for enterprises that process waste, or tackling waste.
5th: The 5th Plenary Meeting on Bridging the Gaps of 3R Infrastructure Towards a Waste-Free Society:
Bridging the gap between 3R infrastructure (reduction, reuse, recycling) is an important step towards achieving a waste-free society. This can be done through a combination of education policies and investment in infrastructure such as recycling sites.
Achieving a waste-free society can be initiated at the local or community level, reached the provincial level and can be implemented nationwide.
6th: Plenary Session on the use of environmentally friendly products instead of disposable plastics:
Law enforcement, which places a burden on the production or use of single-use plastics, can contribute to reducing the use of single-use plastics. At the same time, encouraging the production and use of environmentally friendly products that can replace single-use plastics such as plastic bags, glass, water, metal, metal pipes or wood, etc., raising public awareness is also an important contributing factor. To reduce the use of disposable plastics as well.
8th: Plenary Meeting on the Final Draft of the Second Draft Report on the Status of the 3Rs in Asia and the Pacific, Chakra Economic Promotion in Asia and the Pacific Towards Achieving the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs):
Joint Purpose of the Report on the Status of 3Rs in Asia and the Pacific to Assist the Member States of the 3R Regional Forum and the Chakra Economy in Asia and the Pacific for Improving Decisions Towards Effective Implementation of 3Rs and Utilization of economic resources and economics at the local and national levels.
The forum reviewed the draft report and participants agreed to provide the necessary input by March 31, 2023. The final report will be officially launched at the 12th Asia-Pacific Forum on 3R and Chakra Economy in 2024.
